:ruby
  PageHeader = import("/components/PageHeader.haml")
  DocsPage = import("/components/DocsPage.haml")
  DocsSection = import("/components/DocsSection.haml")

%DocsPage
  %PageHeader{
    kicker: "Plugin Reference",
    title: "HamlPlugin",
    summary: "HamlPlugin turns `.haml` files into Ruby component classes with imports, source maps, companion styles, and optional translations."
  }

  %DocsSection{ title: "Component Output" }
    :markdown
      A Haml module exports a component class as `Default`. The generated class inherits from the configured component base class and renders through the configured factory.

      ```ruby
      Klenod::Build::Plugins::HamlPlugin.new(
        component_base_class: "Example::Component",
        factory: "Example::H",
        variables: {
          global: "@__props",
          class: "Example::Context.current"
        },
        i18n_class: "Example::I18n"
      )
      ```

      Imported component constants can be used directly as Haml tags. `%Button` compiles to a factory call using the imported component class as the tag. Imported TSX custom element descriptors from `JavaScriptPlugin` can also be used as tags, letting Haml render browser custom elements without repeating their generated tag names.

  %DocsSection{ title: "Configured Variables" }
    :markdown
      `variables` optionally maps Ruby variable kinds to framework-owned receiver expressions. Each configured variable becomes indexed access on its receiver.

      ```haml
      %h1= $title
      = if @@request.representation?(:html)
        %p= $summary
      ```

      With the configuration above, these read `(@__props)[:title]`, `(Example::Context.current)[:request]`, and `(@__props)[:summary]`. The example framework sets `@__props` before component initialization and installs request context around rendering.

      The available keys are `global`, `class`, and `instance`, corresponding to `$name`, `@@name`, and `@name`. Unconfigured kinds retain their normal Ruby behavior. Built-in globals such as `$!`, `$1`, and `$LOAD_PATH`, along with underscore-prefixed framework variables such as `@__state`, are preserved.

  %DocsSection{ title: "State Stores" }
    :markdown
      Frameworks can map instance variables to a state object without making Klenod responsible for state behavior:

      ```ruby
      variables: {
        instance: "@__state"
      }
      ```

      `@count`, `@count = 1`, and `@count += 1` then operate on `(@__state)[:count]`. The state object decides whether an assignment changed, how to schedule updates, and how state is serialized.

      Mutable values must be reassigned to notify an indexed state store. Use `@items = [*@items, item]` instead of `@items << item`; the latter mutates the returned array without invoking the store's assignment method.

  %DocsSection{ title: "Context And Lazy Children" }
    :markdown
      Context storage and provider behavior belong to the application framework; Klenod only rewrites the configured variable expression. Child content for component tags is passed to the configured factory as a block, so a framework can resolve component children and named slots lazily after installing inherited context values.

  %DocsSection{ title: "Ruby Blocks And Filters" }
    :markdown
      Top-level `:ruby` defines imports, constants, and methods on the generated component class. Later Ruby script blocks compile into the render method, where request-local helpers from the application framework are available.

      `=` prints a value, `-` runs code silently, and printed Ruby blocks such as `= if condition` render their Haml body.

      Haml `:markdown` filters are delegated to `MarkdownPlugin`. Haml `:css` filters become inline CSS sources collected from the template.

  %DocsSection{ title: "Companions" }
    :markdown
      Haml automatically watches and imports matching CSS companions as `ClassNames`. The companion CSS uses the normal Ruby/Haml CSS path, so class and tag selectors are scoped through `CSSPlugin`.

      ```text
      components/Card.haml
      components/Card.css

      routes/docs/+page.haml
      routes/docs/+page.css
      ```

      Translation companions use `IntlPlugin`. Haml can run without `IntlPlugin`; in that case the generated component receives an empty translation map.

      ```text
      components/Card.intl.en.toml
      components/Card.intl.sv.toml
      ```

      `i18n_class` is optional. When configured, every generated component gets an `I18n` constant initialized with the component class:

      ```ruby
      I18n = Example::I18n.new(self)
      ```

      `i18n_constant` can override the constant name. If omitted, it defaults to `I18n`.

  %DocsSection{ title: "Errors" }
    :markdown
      Generated Ruby includes source map marks. Runtime backtraces can be rewritten to original Haml files and parse errors show Haml source context without requiring users to inspect generated Ruby. CSS companions and inline CSS filters keep their own source maps, so stylesheet errors and browser source maps point back to the original source where possible.
